## Reading 09 — branch disposition

Every way a candidate heading token can be encountered, and why it starts/ends the section,
remains ordinary content, or produces an explicit disagreement:

| Encounter | Structural status | Disposition |
|---|---|---|
| Real level-2 ATX heading with the expected current title, outside fenced code (1a present / 1d repeated) | Section start (first) / ordinary content that breaks the table body (later) | First occurrence opens the section; a repeated one stays inside, ends the governed table body per GFM Example 201, and its table is reported as a further table (SELF-12) |
| Real level-2 ATX heading with the expected next title, outside fenced code (1b absent / 1c present) | Section end | Present: terminates, later rows belong to later sections (1c green). Absent: the section runs to end of document and every later table is a reported further table (1b) |
| Paragraph text carrying heading bytes — prose mention (1e) or inline code span (1f) | Not a heading: the marker is not at line start | Ordinary content; a later contradictory table stays in the section and is reported |
| Heading bytes inside fenced code (1g, 1k, 1n) | Literal code: fence tracking suppresses block recognition | Ordinary code; never starts/ends the section. A mismatched closer (1k) does not exit the fence, so bytes up to the matching closer stay code. Content before the real start heading (1n) is outside the section |
| Escaped `\#` (1h) | Paragraph text: the line starts with `\`, not `#` | Ordinary content; the following table is reported |
| Hash run with no required whitespace `##Current` (1i), over-long run `#######` (1j), four-space indented code (1j2) | Paragraph text / code per the ATX grammar (whitespace-or-EOL, ≤6 hashes, ≤3 spaces indent) | Ordinary content; the following table is reported |
| Blockquote-carried heading bytes `> ## …` (1l) | Quote block, not a top-level section heading | Ordinary content; the following table is reported. (Fences inside blockquotes are likewise not tracked as section-level fences; any rows there break on the quote marker and surface as disagreements rather than silence.) |
| Prose mention of the current title before the real heading (1m) | Paragraph text before the section starts | Outside the section; planted rows there change nothing (green) |

Four branches are quiet, each for a stated reason the invariant allows: 1c/1m/1n drop
nothing that belongs to the section (content outside it by structure); 2e drops fenced lines
that GFM renders as code, so the document asserts no row there; 3b is a valid delimiter variant
(the table parses green); 4b records the row and ignores only excess cells, matching GFM
Example 204's own rendering. Everything else produces at least one message.

Change note on 2d: under round 7 an unclosed fence before the first body row showed only four
reverse-direction messages, because substring section selection never consulted fence state and
the later section headings still delimited. Under structural selection the unclosed fence
swallows the next heading too (GFM renders it as code), so the section runs on and every
downstream table — plus the now-missing Deferred/Export sections — is reported. That is the
louder, GFM-faithful consequence of the same input; the invariant (no silent row loss) holds
either way, and the committed suite pins the closed-fence shapes while this audit records the
unclosed shape explicitly.
